Today I learned that in a system where the output of an Op-Amp is connected to the inverting input, if the voltage coming into the inverting input is higher, the output voltage will lower. If the non-inverting input has a higher voltage coming in, the output voltage will increase. This system will keep attempting to balance the 2 input voltages so that the difference is zero. In the system for the KiCad workshop that was designed(and I probably should self-teach myself KiCad soon), I also learned today that the diode in the system will prevent only current from flowing backwards, and that the capacitor after the first Op-Amp acts sort of like a reference and a battery that keeps the voltage so that the next Op-Amp can accurately measure the input voltage(Since we are measuring an analog signal which can fluctuate). Also, this capacitor will store and hold the highest voltage value that it reaches, if the voltage does drop, the diode blocks the reverse current. The second Op-Amp will also not drain the capacitor.This allows whatever device that is measuring the device to get a steady voltage output recorded from the inital analog input.

